This article presents a framework for addressing general convex minimization problems within a discrete time setting. We introduce a discrete-time mirror descent method designed as a Tikhonov regularization of the continuous-time continuous path gradient flow. Within a Hilbert space $H$ and a fixed time $T$, we investigate the problem defined by $\min_{x \in H} f(x)$ where $f$ is convex but potentially non-smooth or unbounded at certain points, including cases where the domain of $f$ is a convex subset of $H$. In such scenarios, the minimal solution set is denoted as $C := \text{dom}\,f\cap f^{-1}\{0\}$. In the context of a discrete-time approximation $f_\epsilon(t)$ smoothly close in value to $f(t)$, the proposed regularization consists in solving the following optimization problem:
\[
\tag{1}
\begin{aligned}
&\ddot{x}(t) = A(x(t)) \\
&x(0) = x'(0) = 0,
\end{aligned}
\]
where $A(x)$ is a monotone operator and $x(t)$ is a trajectory in a Hilbert space.

